# ============================================================================
# 50-electron-glue.patch - Electron-side exposing of the fp TLS/HTTP2 profile.
#
# Part of the fp-fingerprint patch set. Apply in filename order:
#   00-core.patch -> 10-blink-core.patch -> 20-blink-modules.patch ->
#   30-webrtc.patch -> 40-net-tls.patch -> 50-electron-glue.patch
#
# WHY THIS PATCH EXISTS
#   40-net-tls.patch adds fp_* fields to net::SSLContextConfig and to the
#   network::mojom::SSLConfig / NetworkContextParams mojom messages. Those
#   fields are unreachable from JavaScript without this patch, because
#   Session::SetSSLConfig converts its JS argument through a HAND-WRITTEN gin
#   converter that reads only an explicit whitelist of keys and drops the rest.
#
#   That converter carried the comment:
#       // TODO(nornagon): also support other SSLConfig properties?
#   Every unknown key was therefore silently ignored - the single most
#   dangerous failure mode in a fingerprint system, because the profile then
#   claims a fingerprint it does not produce and nothing reports it. It was
#   caught only because a test asserted that applying a profile CHANGES the
#   observed ClientHello; all profiles initially produced an IDENTICAL JA4.
#
# TWO ENTRY POINTS, DELIBERATELY DIFFERENT
#   TLS  : session.setSSLConfig({ fp* })   - works live, because SSLConfig has
#          an OnSSLConfigUpdated mojo channel to the network service.
#   HTTP2: session.fromPartition(name, { http2Profile: {...} }) - MUST be passed
#          at construction. HttpNetworkSessionParams are read once when the
#          NetworkContext is built, which happens inside fromPartition() before
#          any Session method can run. A post-hoc setter was implemented, wired
#          through mojom, and verified to have NO effect (instrumentation showed
#          ConfigureNetworkContextParams always saw it unset). The setter is
#          kept but LOG(WARNING)s instead of pretending to work.
#
# NOT INCLUDED (generated, not tracked):
#   electron.d.ts is gitignored (generated from the C++ definitions), so the
#   TypeScript declarations for these options are regenerated at build time.
#
# MERGE/UPGRADE GUIDE
#   - electron_api_session.cc, Converter<SSLConfigPtr>: fp_* reads are appended
#     after disabledCipherSuites. If upstream adds SSLConfig fields, append
#     after ours.
#   - electron_api_session.cc, Converter<Http2ProfilePtr>: new converter.
#   - electron_api_session.h: needs the FULL network_context.mojom.h (not
#     -forward) because Http2ProfilePtr is held by value.
#   - electron_browser_context.cc constructor: reads the "http2Profile" option
#     via the same options.Find* pattern as "cache".
#   - JS camelCase (fpCipherList, settingsGrease) maps to mojom snake_case
#     (fp_cipher_list, settings_grease).
# ============================================================================

# --- shell/browser/api/electron_api_session.cc ---
# RANGE: Converter<SSLConfigPtr>.FromV8 (fp_* keys), new
# Converter<Http2ProfilePtr>, Session::SetHttp2Profile, method registration.
# PURPOSE: make both profiles reachable from JS. The SSLConfig converter is
# the whitelist that previously dropped every fp_* key.
# MERGE: HIGH - for a reason that is not about conflicts. The gin converter
#   reads an explicit whitelist and DROPS UNKNOWN KEYS WITHOUT ANY DIAGNOSTIC
#   (upstream even shipped the comment "TODO(nornagon): also support other
#   SSLConfig properties?"). So if upstream adds a field to SSLConfig and we do
#   not, or if a key is renamed on our side only, the build compiles, the
#   profile applies, and the value is silently absent. That was a real bug: it
#   was caught only by a test asserting that applying a profile CHANGES the
#   observed ClientHello - all profiles initially produced an IDENTICAL JA4.
#   After any change here, verify per key (Client/test-tls-keys.js), never by
#   aggregate. Also: fp_* reads are appended after disabledCipherSuites, so if
#   upstream adds SSLConfig fields, append after OURS.
#   The JS camelCase -> mojom snake_case mapping (fpCipherList ->
#   fp_cipher_list, settingsGrease -> settings_grease) lives here and must match
#   Client/fp-schema.js; a mismatch is not a compile error.
